{"connection_id": "BC-d3cfe5ead57b29a39d6f", "ref": "Augustine, Letter 130.9", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "God knows needs before spoken prayer", "note": "The commentary says God knows an inward as well as uttered reading. Augustine explains that prayer-words do not inform God, who already knows human need, but exercise the pray-er’s desire; this is a patristic account of the same premise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f2490e9d1ffd82c9b97", "ref": "Cyprian, On the Lord's Prayer 4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "quiet prayer; God hears the heart", "note": "Cyprian teaches that prayer should be quiet because God hears the heart rather than the volume of a voice. This directly interprets the commentary's claim that God knows recitation whether it is on the tongue or in the heart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-36b913a83ec398692540", "ref": "Didache 8:2-3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fixed prayer and God's name", "note": "The Didache prescribes the Lord's Prayer three times daily, including the petition that God's name be hallowed; this gives a concrete Christian practice joining repeated recitation, the divine name and prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fde56be4dc50dd787b6", "ref": "Justin Martyr, First Apology 67.3-4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "scripture read in assembly", "note": "Justin describes the memoirs of the apostles or prophets being read in the Sunday gathering, followed by instruction; this is a specific Christian public-reading practice alongside the commentary's recitation and text-in-hand reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87ee4e7e84b2e2c6b028", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.11.24", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "liturgical remembrance in enacted worship", "note": "The commentary joins dhikr to prayer. Paul's Eucharistic command, \"do this in remembrance of me,\" makes remembrance a repeated Christian worship-act, though its object and rite differ from remembering God's name in 87:15."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1a89096f1e366d2be505", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "praying with spirit and mind", "note": "Paul distinguishes and then joins praying and singing with the spirit and with the mind; this is a particularly close Christian formulation alongside the commentary's tongue-and-heart remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-44657c1877ba3bff47f5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer and song with spirit and mind", "note": "The commentary distinguishes raised sound from an inward mode of worship. Paul resolves a comparable pairing by praying and singing with both spirit and mind, bringing cognition into voiced prayer and song."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8f7302b8286def7c802", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mysteries spoken to God", "note": "Paul says a person speaking in a tongue speaks mysteries to God and not to people; this gives a precise case of utterance hidden from human hearers yet addressed to God, who knows what the commentary says is hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a49150ea8226eac128e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.25", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "secrets of the heart disclosed", "note": "Paul says a visitor's heart secrets become manifest through prophecy, leading to worship; this connects inward hidden matter, public speech and prayer in one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-885d7ed48c1715db1673", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.25", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "secrets of heart disclosed in worship", "note": "The commentary connects hidden knowledge, audible word, and prayer. Paul says an outsider's heart-secrets are disclosed and that person then worships God and declares God's presence, a tightly related congregational scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e317401bd00eb1c7638", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.14.28", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "communal silence and speech to self and God", "note": "The commentary balances voiced and internal devotion. Paul tells an uninterpreted speaker to keep silence in the assembly while speaking to himself and to God, distinguishing public sound from God-directed speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a976986851c648639d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.6", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "planting, watering, and God giving growth", "note": "The commentary makes pasture's emergence God's act despite rain and soil. Paul distinguishes human planting and watering from God who gives the growth, a Christian agricultural image of the same agency distinction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-079641bbe17d84e298c1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hidden motives disclosed", "note": "Paul says the Lord will bring hidden things of darkness to light and disclose the purposes of hearts; this fuses the commentary's concealed matters with its inward remembrance and divine knowing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b5e2d38b271cee1e622", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hidden darkness and counsels of hearts disclosed", "note": "The commentary joins concealed things emerging from cover with the knowledge of inner reality. Paul says the Lord will bring hidden darkness to light and disclose the counsels of hearts, uniting both movements in judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c55a67fd303d2775f33a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.4.13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public reading of scripture", "note": "Paul directs Timothy to devote himself to public reading, exhortation and teaching; this is a specific Christian liturgical counterpart to the commentary's reading aloud and by sight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01235aa8edd7c8c67c22", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unapproachable light that no human can see", "note": "The commentary says overwhelming light can function like a cover to sight. First Timothy places God in unapproachable light whom no human has seen or can see, a theological extension of visual excess beyond the sun-image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15bf243933024b086176", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "veil over Scripture reading", "note": "Paul interprets Moses' veil as remaining over readers of the old covenant until Christ removes it; this Christian reading makes the commentary's conjunction of recitation, concealment and disclosure a live interpretive question."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d65a39d8750323a8fff7", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "veil over the heart", "note": "Paul says a veil lies over the heart when Moses is read; this explicitly joins textual reading to inward concealment, the two fields the commentary brings together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69856ad6dc89c1e683dc", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.15", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses read while a veil lies on the heart", "note": "The commentary connects reading, heart, and covering. Paul says that when Moses is read a veil lies on the heart, a Christian interpretive image in which scriptural reading itself can remain interiorly covered."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3a7ae9d43db6118b17a", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "turning removes the veil", "note": "Paul says the veil is removed when one turns to the Lord; this gives a Christian interpretive account of concealment as something lifted through turning toward God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da992a41356a71811c18", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spirit-written letter on human hearts", "note": "The commentary contrasts a text looked at with one retained in the heart. Paul calls the Corinthians Christ's letter, written by the Spirit on human hearts rather than with ink, making inward inscription a reading-image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df79f2e13cca83d2b69e", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.3.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brightness that Israel could not gaze at", "note": "The commentary's secondary image is sight defeated by excessive light. Paul says Israel could not gaze at Moses' face because of its glory, making visual incapacity caused by radiance explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50d938da0796b583f4ef", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.1.24", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prayer to the Lord as knower of all hearts", "note": "The commentary says inward realities are known to God. Before choosing Matthias, the disciples pray to the Lord specifically as the knower of everyone's heart, making the claim part of communal discernment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd6448b3d8c091bfcbc0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.22.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "light prevents sight", "note": "Paul says he could not see because of the glory of the light; this directly matches the commentary's secondary observation that too much light can itself become a veil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-290f31e11405deb85d15", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.22.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unable to see because of brilliant light", "note": "The commentary's cehra image is an eye unable to see in sun. Paul says he could not see because of the brilliance of the heavenly light, a narrative instance of sight overwhelmed by light."}

{"connection_id": "BC-7a5ea0826f36a1e1e818", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.8.30", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "scripture read aloud", "note": "Philip hears the Ethiopian reading Isaiah and asks whether he understands; this joins voiced recitation from a written text to inward comprehension, two aspects the commentary holds together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2c23fbdbc677cb2f97c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.8.30", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "audible Scripture reading and understanding", "note": "The commentary joins recitation with being aware of what is said. Philip hears the Ethiopian reading and asks whether he understands what he reads, a concrete Christian scene of oral scripture and understanding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d1245eec6121d6fa365", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.1.26", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mystery hidden through ages now manifested", "note": "The commentary makes the hidden something God can bring out. Colossians calls Christ's mystery hidden through ages and generations but now manifested to the saints, a Christian revelation-history form of that movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ea6730b0b6097fdd571", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word dwelling and song", "note": "The message of Christ is to dwell richly among the community, which teaches and sings psalms with gratitude in the heart; this joins remembered sacred words, voiced praise and inward devotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-234a460c98083fcb9ed4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word dwelling richly; songs in hearts", "note": "The commentary connects recitation, remembrance, and worship in tongue and heart. Colossians commands Christ's word to dwell richly and joins teaching with sung worship in hearts, preserving that combined practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-26dd2df5ee59ba05181c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.19", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "singing in the heart", "note": "The community speaks to one another in psalms and sings to the Lord in its heart; this pairs voiced religious speech with the inward orientation that the commentary assigns to remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ab7c9f34a20b9a674dc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.19", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "spoken psalms and melody in heart", "note": "The commentary distinguishes audible speech from inward remembrance without separating them. Ephesians pairs speaking psalms and hymns with singing and making melody in the heart to the Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dd6951b351ef5971a1a8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word reaches the heart", "note": "Hebrews describes God's word as discerning thoughts and intentions of the heart; this connects recited divine speech to inward content, a specific bridge to the commentary's reading and knowing scenes."}
